#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Root proof-review additions after freeze. Does not touch transcription."""
from pathlib import Path
from datetime import datetime, timezone
import json
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'SP33_CW_156-5_mathematical_checks.md'
s=p.read_text()
s=s.replace('the F-stratum', 'the F-stratum')
a=r'''For clarity in that converse, support saturation is already automatic from the raw subfamily inclusion: each ambient member is a union of ambient strata. If the smaller stratum indexed by \(C\in F\) equals the ambient stratum indexed by \(B\in G\), then \(C_G^\circ\subseteq C_F^\circ=B_G^\circ\), because every proper smaller-family member is an ambient member. The nonempty \(C_G^\circ\) and disjointness of ambient strata force \(C=B\). This supplies the equality-of-members step in the preceding paragraph rather than assuming it from equality of partitions alone.

The four compatibility criteria on scans008–012 can be checked explicitly. Put \(D=F\cap G\) and \(T=|F|\cap|G|\). If \(D\) is down-closed in both and \(|D|=T\) (condition b), a point in the overlap has the same least member in both: choose a common member through it, and use down-closure to see that both least members belong to \(D\), then compare them in both directions. If \(B\in G\) lies inside \(A\in F\), choose \(x\in B_G^\circ\); some member of \(D\) contains \(x\), and leastness plus down-closure puts \(B\) in \(D\). Thus both families are down-closed in their union, their strata are unchanged, and that union is admissible, proving a. The reverse implication follows by working in the common upper figure; a-prime is equivalent to a by the same downset-union argument.

Condition c says that \(T\) is closed for both and that the members contained in it coincide. Such closed sets are unions of the corresponding lower members, so c is equivalent to b. Condition d gives this locally on every \(A\cap B\): their union \(T\) is closed for both, and if \(C\in F\) is contained in \(T\), take \(x\in C_F^\circ\) and \(B\in G\) through \(x\). The \(F\)-closed intersection \(C\cap B\) contains \(x\), hence contains \(C\); local agreement then puts \(C\) in \(G\). The symmetric argument proves c, while a implies d by restriction inside the common upper figure. This proves a, a-prime, b, c, d equivalent without importing the sweep's finite computation.

'''
marker='For compatible families'
if 'For clarity in that converse' not in s:s=s.replace(marker,a+marker)
a=r'''The marginal reconstruction from \(M\) on scan017 also follows. Send \(F\) to all multistrates below it; this is down-closed in \(M\) and pairwise compatible. Conversely, for a down-closed pairwise compatible subset \(D\subseteq M\), form \(F=\bigcup D\) by P05. If \(A\in F\), some \(X\in D\) contains it and its principal lower subfamily is unchanged in \(F\), so \(F_A=X_A\le X\) lies in \(D\). Hence the multistrates of \(F\) are exactly \(D\), even though \(M\) is disconnected.

'''
marker='This complete atomic Boolean algebra'
if 'The marginal reconstruction from' not in s:s=s.replace(marker,a+marker)
a=r'''The continuity NB on scan021 follows as well. If support inclusion holds and every \(A\cap B\) is \(F\)-closed, then \(B\cap|F|=\bigcup_{A\in F}(A\cap B)\) is \(F\)-closed for every \(B\in G\). The inverse image of any union of \(G\)-members is therefore closed, and the support inclusion is continuous. Conversely, continuity makes those inverse images closed and hence their intersections with \(A\) closed. For these preorder topologies this is exactly preorder preservation: a least closed neighbourhood of a point maps into the least closed neighbourhood of its image, and conversely order preservation pulls downsets back to downsets. This verifies the marginal assertion rather than imposing it as an additional axiom.

'''
marker='The independent finite computation verifies the two principal formulations'
if 'The continuity NB' not in s:s=s.replace(marker,a+marker)
s=s.replace('This checks the complete inverse-image sentence on scan025.', 'This checks the inverse-image sentence on scan025 with “modérée” interpreted as saturated, or with the finite-figure qualification of scan006.')
a=r'''
The Boolean qualification has an explicit infinite counterexample. Let \(F=\{\{n\}:n\in\mathbb N\}\), and let \(G\) consist of the even and odd subsets of \(\mathbb N\). Both are admissible and \(F\ll G\): the map from the discrete singleton index set to the two-point discrete index set is parity. The even set is a member of \(G\), hence is \(G\)-constructible in the ordinary Boolean sense, but it is not in the finite–cofinite Boolean algebra generated by \(F\). Thus preservation of saturated subsets must not silently be promoted to preservation of finite-Boolean constructibility for arbitrary infinite figures. This is a counterexample to that strengthened interpretation, not an alteration of the text's terminology.
'''
if 'The Boolean qualification has' not in s:s+=a
p.write_text(s)
now=datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S UTC')
m=p.parent/'records/mathematical_proofs.json'
d=json.loads(m.read_text());d['reviewed_and_extended_utc']=now;d['extensions']=['P05 induced-partition converse and all compatibility criteria','P08 reconstruction from closed compatible multistrates','P11 continuity NB','P13 infinite finite-Boolean counterexample'];d['source_sha256']=__import__('hashlib').sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest();m.write_text(json.dumps(d,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
with (p.parent/'progress.log').open('a') as f:f.write(f'mathematical proof review completed {now}; P05 P08 P11 P13 expanded; no frozen file changed\n')
print(now)
